when George Lucas came up with the idea  of Star Wars in the mid-1970s setting an  adventure of this scope and outerspace  had never been done before so how did he  get the film to look so good when there  were no visual effects companies up to  the challenge at the time he started his  own Industrial Light &amp; Magic was formed  to handle the special effects in Star  Wars and they're still revolutionising  the industry with their work let's peek  behind the scenes at what Star Wars  movies look like before they work their  magic a new hope the original trilogy is  known for its use of practical effects  but in truth those films relied on  visual trickery just as much as their  successors sprawling CGI backgrounds  were not an option when the first Star  Wars movie was being made so the effects  artists at ILM came up with some  ingenious alternatives matte glass  paintings were painstakingly created by  hands and then added to scenes later but  a different technique was needed to  bring the movies many spaceships to life  former ILM frontman John Dykstra told  den of geek back in the days of Star  Wars we kind of walked into an empty  warehouse and set on the floor and went  how are we going to do this  the producers Gary Kurtz of Fox and  George Lucas took an incredible risk by  listening to what myself and my  collaborators had to say with regard to  how to do this because we were inventing  this stuff from scratch when it came to  Han Solo's Millennium Falcon Dykstra  decided to create a model and film it in  front of a blue screen using an early  computer-controlled camera which allowed  his team to add all of the outerspace  details in Post The Empire Strikes Back  Dykstra and his team won an Oscar for  the revolutionary work on Star Wars but  Lucas chose not to bring Dykstra along  when he moved his base of operations to  San Francisco it was here that ILM got  to work on the next movie but despite  the success of the first film budget  restraints were still a problem when it  came to making the Empire Strikes Back  as VFX supervisor Richard Edlund later  pointed out computer graphics were  extremely expensive very slow and the  resolution was always a problem  luckily for Edlund and Lucas the team  they'd assembled proved more than  capable of delivering results within  their me  the film's cinematographer mark Virgo  compared this era in cinematic history  to the lunar program referring to the  period as an amazing cooperative of  artists imagination optics engineering  and home-brewed software Return of the  Jedi  by the time 1983 s Return of the Jedi  went into production the ILM crew had  found their groove Master of Puppets  Phil Tippett was brought in to head up a  creature shop that ultimately created  the Ewoks and the rest of the effects  work was divided between Richard Edlund  Ken Ralston and Dennis Muren among the  jobs that fell to Mirren was the speeder  bike chase one of the hardest sequences  in the film with 100 total effect shots  Mir and explained because the bikes were  going to be blue screened the  backgrounds didn't require that many  different shots to do because we could  use them over and over again I got the  idea to do it with a Steadicam  I called Garrett Brown up who invented  it and he flew out from back east and we  did a test out in Samuel Taylor Park out  here with him just walking along with  the camera shooting one frame a second  and the thing worked Muller and Browne  spent several days in the woods  just filming trees from different angles  then matched the footage up when they  went back to the studio to film the  actors in front of the blue screens the  Phantom Menace George Lucas's  long-awaited return to the Star Wars  universe didn't fail financially but it  did let down a number of fans who were  quick to turn on new CGI character Jar  Jar Binks  despite the overwhelmingly negative  reaction to the character I'm at best  the man who played the Gungan is proud  of the fact that he broke new ground  with Jar Jar he explained one of the  biggest reasons I took it was because of  the challenge it hadn't been done before  there was no Andy Serkis and Gollum no  Navi from Avatar I was working with  George to pioneer this new character  form of acting and storytelling unlike  best the actor who played Darth Maul  only has fond memories of the fans of  menace his first experience on a movie  set martial artist and actor ray park  was initially only brought in to film  some test footage but Lucas liked his  work so much that he decided to cast him  as the Sith Lord park's primary  Fighting's  is wushu but he also practiced  gymnastics and ballet in preparation for  his big fight scene with obi-wan Kenobi  and qui-gon jinn once the choreography  was worked out the scene was filmed in  front of a custom blue set and handed  off to ILM just one of the many  sequences they were required to complete  Attack of the Clones  the ongoing advances in CGI meant that  Lucas could set stories in parts of his  galaxy that were previously inaccessible  which he took full advantage of an  attack of the clones  he explained that was the fun part of  writing the project I wasn't limited  whatever my imagination could come up  with I would just put down on the page  and say we'll worry about this later  animation director Rob Coleman was  initially under the impression that some  of the cityscapes and backgrounds Lucas  required for Episode two were going to  have some practical elements but he soon  learned this wasn't the case of the  2,000 effect shots and attack of the  clones around half include completely  computer-generated characters a team of  60 animators worked alongside 340 ILM  artists and technicians to render some  of the most well known Star Wars phases  in CGI including a certain little green  Jedi Master Rob Coleman explained the  biggest technical challenge for me was  animating Yoda that was due to Frank Oz  having created that character in the  Empire Strikes Back as a puppet  this time Yoda is much more active and  prominent he actually runs and has a  lightsaber battle  Yodas duel with Count Dooku was one of  the film's big visual effects set pieces  but it required some human input from  Christopher Lee who was one of the many  cast members forced to work with blue  screens for long periods of time Revenge  of the Sith according to Ian McDermott  who played sheev Palpatine the use of  green screen was really ramped up for  the third and final film in the Lucas  prequel trilogy which had the most  effect shots yet he told The Telegraph  so much of the filming took place in  front of a green screen I used to feel  great sympathy for you and in Hayden  because they had so much fighting to do  and quite often had to fight imagined  enemies  George often hadn't decided on the  details of the film so you and Hayden  didn't know if they were filming an  ironic moment or whether they were about  to  episode three also introduced us to  General Grievous the lightsaber wielding  cyborg who held the title of supreme  commander of the droid army during the  Clone Wars upon receiving the design for  the character animation director Rob  Coleman new ILM were going to have a  tough job breathing life into this one  he explained since his movement was so  restricted we decided to have him talk  with his hands because although there is  a grill pattern where his mouth should  be there are no articulating bits for  his mouth no lip sync or facial  expressions no brow to move no  indication of what he is feeling or  thinking grievous wasn't the only  villain without eyebrows in this movie  it's an episode 3 that we get to see  Anakin Skywalker become Darth Vader  after he's badly burned in a battle with  obi-wan a scene which had its own  effects challenges Hayden Christensen  wasn't originally going to be the person  in the iconic suit but in the end he  convinced Lucas to let him play the part  I begged them clean it and they were  nice enough to build the Darth Vader so  to actually fit me  the force awakens after working with JJ  Abrams on his Star Trek reboot visual  effects supervisor Roger Guyot joined  the director going into the force  awakens and the Paris partnership proved  as fruitful as ever episode 7 made over  two billion dollars globally while Guyot  and his team were nominated for an  Academy Award for their work much of  which incorporated practical effects in  particular real models were often used  for the little bb-8 droid guyot told our  two BFFs  from the beginning we knew we wanted  bb-8 to be as practical as possible so  that the actors could interact with him  and of course JJ could direct him on set  removing the puppeteers from shots later  on proved tricky but guyot had nothing  but praise for them despite all the  effort that went into making bb-8 real  there were still a number of scenes in  which the character had to be rendered  digitally out of necessity around a  third of all shots in fact obviously  some of the characters couldn't just be  done physically and and those occasions  judge I was more than happy to push the  digital technology of fully digital  actors for all the practical work guyot  estimates that the force awakens  contains around 2,100 effect shots  robe one a Star Wars story the first  Star Wars spin-off went in a new  direction in terms of both narrative and  style and most critics were impressed  with the result  one of the major talking points was the  digital resurrection of Peter Cushing as  grand moff tarkin whose character was  portrayed by guy Henry VIII emotion  capture VFX Supervisor John Knoll was  able to track down a cast of Cushing  Space made for the 1984 movie top secret  which gave them a huge head start when  it came to recreating the late actor's  likeness the process took a whopping 18  months to complete and was done with the  permission of cushing's estate of course  Tarkin wasn't the only CG character in  row 1 to create the droid k2s oh the  filmmakers fitted alan tudyk with a  motion capture suit and then digitally  added the robot's body later to get to  dicks face at the appropriate level for  the towering robot they took two  different approaches in some scenes  Dudek Wars stilts while he interacted  with the other actors  if the shot was too dangerous to you  stilts he wore a backpack with a paper  cutout of K two's head positioned  several feet above his own now that's  dedication  there's no way to Madinah the last Jedi  JJ Abrams spoke at length about using as  many practical effects as possible in  the force awakens in order to recreate  the feeling of the original trilogy and  his successor Ryan Johnson adopted the  same attitude when he took the reins for  the last Jedi Neal Scanlan returned to  head up the creature shop and this time  they were tasked with creating a new  breed of animal called a vortex a fox  like inhabitants of the planet crate  Scanlon told Entertainment Weekly the  idea is that these wonderful sort of  feral creatures had lived on this planet  and had consumed the planet's surface  and as such had become crystalline to  get an idea of how such a creature might  move they fitted a small dog with a suit  adorned with drinking straws the next  step was creating an animatronic puppet  that the casts could interact with in  scenes but much like the bb-8 build it  wasn't possible to use it 100% of the  time static models complete with 25,000  individual crystals were built for the  benefit of the VFX  who were able to scan them and render  the creatures however Johnson wanted  thanks for watching click the looper  icon to subscribe to our YouTube channel  plus check out all this cool stuff we  know you love to  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

